1
00:00:01,901 --> 00:00:05,583
Hi, this is team forty five's project for phase four.

2
00:00:05,583 --> 00:00:08,988
We built a database for the ICC cricket rankings,

3
00:00:08,988 --> 00:00:15,310
and the web app you see on the left is modelled on the rankings
section of the ICC website.

4
00:00:15,874 --> 00:00:18,721
It's MySQL eight with a Flask backend.

5
00:00:18,721 --> 00:00:23,589
On the right I've got the MySQL client open on the same database,

6
00:00:23,589 --> 00:00:26,510
so you can see what changes underneath.

7
00:00:27,064 --> 00:00:33,751
All of this data came from the ICC site itself. The ranking tables at
three different dates,

8
00:00:33,751 --> 00:00:38,186
the player profiles, and real scorecards from recent matches.

9
00:00:38,739 --> 00:00:43,702
Let me quickly go through the main pages. Player rankings,

10
00:00:44,254 --> 00:00:45,938
the matches page,

11
00:00:46,503 --> 00:00:48,341
and the team pages.

12
00:00:48,903 --> 00:00:56,772
Okay, the queries. The first one is the team rankings table. This is
men's Test cricket.

13
00:00:57,330 --> 00:01:01,816
South Africa are on top with a rating of a hundred and twenty four,

14
00:01:01,816 --> 00:01:06,838
from two thousand eight hundred and sixty points over twenty three
matches.

15
00:01:06,838 --> 00:01:10,118
That's exactly what the ICC site shows right now.

16
00:01:10,118 --> 00:01:14,068
The rating and the position are both calculated in the SQL.

17
00:01:14,635 --> 00:01:20,922
We can also go back in time. If I pick an older rank date, we get that
week's table,

18
00:01:20,922 --> 00:01:23,617
and the arrows show how teams moved.

19
00:01:24,175 --> 00:01:28,413
And these tabs switch between men and women and the three formats.

20
00:01:28,966 --> 00:01:35,938
Query two is player rankings. Here's men's ODI batting, with Shubman
Gill at number one.

21
00:01:36,499 --> 00:01:39,434
The all rounder table isn't stored at all.

22
00:01:39,434 --> 00:01:44,465
It's worked out as batting points times bowling points, over a
thousand,

23
00:01:44,465 --> 00:01:48,589
which is the ICC's own formula, and it matches their table.

24
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:53,423
Three, search. If I type just k o h,

25
00:01:53,977 --> 00:01:57,384
it finds Virat Kohli from part of his name.

26
00:01:57,936 --> 00:02:03,703
It also searches roles and teams. So keeper gives us the wicket
keepers.

27
00:02:04,253 --> 00:02:09,007
Query four is the player profile. Let's open Shubman Gill.

28
00:02:09,562 --> 00:02:15,446
His age comes from his date of birth, and here are his current ranks
in every table he's in.

29
00:02:16,011 --> 00:02:21,492
Further down is his rating history with the change each time, and his
career runs,

30
00:02:21,492 --> 00:02:23,431
added up from the scorecards.

31
00:02:23,995 --> 00:02:27,699
Five is the team page. This is India.

32
00:02:28,262 --> 00:02:33,510
You can see when they got status in each format, their win and loss
record,

33
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:35,119
and the top performers.

34
00:02:35,684 --> 00:02:40,600
And the rating trend for each format, with the change between
snapshots.

35
00:02:41,157 --> 00:02:46,480
Six, the matches list. I'll filter it to completed ODIs.

36
00:02:47,032 --> 00:02:52,216
Opening a match gives the full scorecard, with every player's batting
and bowling.

37
00:02:52,772 --> 00:02:58,481
Seven is head to head. India against New Zealand, across all formats.

38
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:04,076
Eight, player comparison. Gill and Kohli side by side.

39
00:03:04,631 --> 00:03:08,338
Nine shows the biggest movers between two rank dates.

40
00:03:08,338 --> 00:03:11,694
These are the Test batters who climbed the most.

41
00:03:12,253 --> 00:03:16,844
Ten is a series report. For India's ODI series in New Zealand,

42
00:03:16,844 --> 00:03:22,916
we get the top scorers and wicket takers, and each team's rating
before and after.

43
00:03:23,476 --> 00:03:28,689
Eleven is the stats page, with the highest, lowest and average
ratings,

44
00:03:28,689 --> 00:03:31,773
and how many ranked teams each member has.

45
00:03:32,334 --> 00:03:38,605
And twelve, on the admin page, counts how many ratings each analyst
published per month,

46
00:03:38,605 --> 00:03:40,813
grouped under their supervisor.

47
00:03:41,366 --> 00:03:47,651
Now the updates. First an insert. Ireland don't have a Test rating
yet,

48
00:03:47,651 --> 00:03:50,395
so this query comes back empty.

49
00:03:50,958 --> 00:03:55,587
I'll publish one for today, ten matches and three hundred points.

50
00:03:56,137 --> 00:04:00,498
It's saved, and running the same query again shows the new row.

51
00:04:01,059 --> 00:04:04,425
Ireland now show up in the men's Test table too.

52
00:04:04,991 --> 00:04:12,118
Second insert, a player rating. These are Abhishek Sharma's T20
batting ratings right now.

53
00:04:12,681 --> 00:04:16,097
Let me try a rating of fifteen hundred first.

54
00:04:17,551 --> 00:04:21,433
MySQL rejects it, because of the check constraint.

55
00:04:21,433 --> 00:04:25,083
Ratings have to be between zero and a thousand.

56
00:04:25,644 --> 00:04:30,737
With eight ninety one it goes through, and the query now shows today's
rating.

57
00:04:33,143 --> 00:04:39,883
The third insert adds a new player. Player nine hundred thousand and
one doesn't exist yet.

58
00:04:40,435 --> 00:04:44,619
If I enter him as female but put him in the Indian men's team,

59
00:04:51,724 --> 00:04:55,351
a trigger catches the mismatch, and nothing gets saved,

60
00:04:55,351 --> 00:04:58,581
because the three inserts run in one transaction.

61
00:04:59,147 --> 00:05:05,428
With the right details it works. The player, his role and his team
spell are all in.

62
00:05:13,085 --> 00:05:19,844
Now updates. In this match India beat New Zealand, and neither team's
rating is flagged.

63
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:23,575
If I try to mark both teams as winners,

64
00:05:24,130 --> 00:05:28,337
the trigger refuses it, since the two results have to agree.

65
00:05:28,889 --> 00:05:32,956
A consistent change is fine. The results are swapped now,

66
00:05:32,956 --> 00:05:37,593
and both teams' latest ratings are marked as needing a recompute.

67
00:05:38,602 --> 00:05:42,149
That flag shows up on the rankings table as well.

68
00:05:42,712 --> 00:05:48,680
Next, retiring a player. Abhishek Sharma hasn't retired from anything.

69
00:05:49,236 --> 00:05:53,197
I'll retire him from T20 internationals as of today.

70
00:05:53,787 --> 00:05:59,812
He's gone from the current T20 batting table, but all his old ratings
are still kept.

71
00:06:00,376 --> 00:06:06,366
Changing a player's team. Our new player is with India, with an open
spell.

72
00:06:06,925 --> 00:06:14,122
When I move him to Nepal, the India spell gets an end date and a new
one starts, both in one go.

73
00:06:14,686 --> 00:06:18,710
Now ending a team's status. Zimbabwe hold Test status,

74
00:06:18,710 --> 00:06:23,553
and you can see the gap from two thousand six is kept as history.

75
00:06:24,112 --> 00:06:30,975
I'll end their Test status today. The end date is set, and their
latest Test rating is flagged.

76
00:06:31,756 --> 00:06:35,544
And if I try to publish a new Test rating for them now,

77
00:06:37,438 --> 00:06:41,445
it's rejected, because they no longer hold the status.

78
00:06:42,006 --> 00:06:47,742
Correcting a rating. India's ODI rating from the match change is still
flagged.

79
00:06:48,293 --> 00:06:53,018
After I fix the points and save, the update clears the flag.

80
00:06:53,572 --> 00:06:59,431
Last, the deletes. Match two seven four six six four is only
scheduled,

81
00:06:59,431 --> 00:07:01,990
and the other one is completed.

82
00:07:02,553 --> 00:07:05,246
Deleting the scheduled match works.

83
00:07:05,803 --> 00:07:08,758
But the completed one is refused by a trigger.

84
00:07:08,758 --> 00:07:12,740
The query confirms the scheduled match and its teams are gone,

85
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:15,244
and the completed match is still there.

86
00:07:15,804 --> 00:07:20,999
Deleting a rating. Here's the Ireland Test row we added earlier.

87
00:07:21,550 --> 00:07:25,534
Once it's deleted, the same query is empty again.

88
00:07:26,087 --> 00:07:29,134
And deleting a player. Gill can't be deleted,

89
00:07:29,134 --> 00:07:34,009
because the foreign keys protect players with ratings and match
history.

90
00:07:34,569 --> 00:07:39,196
The player we just added has no history, so that delete goes through,

91
00:07:39,196 --> 00:07:41,944
and his role and team spells go with him.

92
00:07:42,506 --> 00:07:50,468
That's everything. All of these queries are plain parameterised SQL,
run through PyMySQL.

93
00:07:50,468 --> 00:07:52,257
Thanks for watching.
